1
00:00:52,134 --> 00:00:53,886
- Excuse me.
- Sure.

2
00:01:12,071 --> 00:01:13,906
Oh wait, now he's crying.

3
00:01:15,783 --> 00:01:17,118
- Let me call you back, okay?
- Here you go.

4
00:01:17,243 --> 00:01:18,995
- Thank you.
- Sure.

5
00:01:45,146 --> 00:01:47,481
"Did you see that?
Oh my God!"

6
00:02:06,208 --> 00:02:10,463
It was right behind me here.
That's where tragedy struck earlier today.

7
00:02:10,588 --> 00:02:14,091
- There's been a bombing incident.
- Morning, Jane.

8
00:02:14,216 --> 00:02:17,845
Cab to a crime scene?
That's very cosmopolitan of you.

9
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:20,514
- Well, my car wouldn't start.
- Well, of course it wouldn't.

10
00:02:21,515 --> 00:02:23,017
How was your date
with agent Pike?

11
00:02:23,142 --> 00:02:26,103
- It was okay.
- Just okay?

12
00:02:26,354 --> 00:02:27,605
It went well.

13
00:02:28,272 --> 00:02:31,192
Good, huh? Oh, well, that's great.
That's great. Excellent.

14
00:02:31,317 --> 00:02:33,653
Hey. The hospital says
there's only one death.

15
00:02:33,778 --> 00:02:35,696
He was right next to the bomb
when it went off.

16
00:02:35,821 --> 00:02:37,740
- Could have been a lot worse.
- How many injuries?

17
00:02:37,865 --> 00:02:40,451
Four, but only one serious.
A homeless man.

18
00:02:40,576 --> 00:02:42,453
- They think he's gonna pull through.
- That's lucky.

19
00:02:42,953 --> 00:02:44,830
Yeah. The eyewitnesses agree
that the bomb went off

20
00:02:44,955 --> 00:02:47,083
in front of the kiosk
just after the bus pulled out

21
00:02:47,208 --> 00:02:49,210
but so far, no one's been able
to I.D. a bomber.

22
00:02:49,335 --> 00:02:50,461
Well, he probably got on the bus.

23
00:02:50,586 --> 00:02:52,630
Did the first responders
get a list of the passengers?

24
00:02:52,755 --> 00:02:54,256
A few.
It was chaos.

25
00:02:54,382 --> 00:02:57,093
We're hoping to pull some
security footage from the area.

26
00:02:57,218 --> 00:03:01,013
Anything you find, make sure you share it
with the joint terrorist task force.

27
00:03:01,222 --> 00:03:03,224
They're gonna look at their crimes
and see if there's anything that fit

28
00:03:03,349 --> 00:03:05,434
- with what we have here.
- You think this is terrorists?

29
00:03:05,559 --> 00:03:07,436
- Well, we can't rule it out.
- Well, I think we can.

30
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:09,230
Someone just wanted
the dead guy dead.

31
00:03:09,355 --> 00:03:11,232
- And why do you think that?
- Because he's dead.

32
00:03:11,357 --> 00:03:14,652
I mean, look at this place. Bomber could
of killed a dozen people if he wanted to.

33
00:03:14,777 --> 00:03:16,696
But, nope, this guy
just bumped off one.

34
00:03:16,821 --> 00:03:19,365
Pretty ineffective terrorism,
if you ask me.

35
00:03:19,657 --> 00:03:21,617
Would be a good way
to bury a homicide.

36
00:03:21,742 --> 00:03:25,079
Nonetheless, we are gonna look at this
as a possible terrorist attack

37
00:03:25,371 --> 00:03:28,749
until we have conclusive evidence
that shows us something else, okay?

38
00:03:28,874 --> 00:03:32,628
Call the NSA and see if they heard
any chatter about an attack, okay?

39
00:03:32,753 --> 00:03:36,298
Sir, we found pieces from the case
the charge was in when it exploded.

40
00:03:36,424 --> 00:03:37,675
We also found this.

41
00:03:38,342 --> 00:03:39,677
A bit strange, actually.

42
00:03:39,802 --> 00:03:41,429
- What is it?
- It's blanketing material.

43
00:03:41,554 --> 00:03:43,848
It appears to have been placed
above and beneath the charge.

44
00:03:43,973 --> 00:03:46,308
- And why's that strange?
- It would have dampened the explosion.

45
00:03:46,434 --> 00:03:49,812
Usually, a bomber wants
a bigger boom, you know? Not this one.

46
00:03:49,937 --> 00:03:53,691
This device was designed to be lethal
only to someone situated right next to it.

47
00:03:54,525 --> 00:03:55,943
I never saw that before.

48
00:03:59,029 --> 00:04:01,449
Dead guy.
Boom.

49
00:04:16,712 --> 00:04:18,172
"It's 9:00."

50
00:04:20,048 --> 00:04:21,675
Did you just tell me
what time it is?

51
00:04:23,677 --> 00:04:25,637
- Excuse me?
- Somebody just said the time.

52
00:04:25,763 --> 00:04:28,682
- Oh. Yeah, my computer.
- "It's 9:00."

53
00:04:28,849 --> 00:04:30,976
{\an8}I've programmed it to announce
the time every quarter-hour.

54
00:04:31,101 --> 00:04:32,811
{\an8}I think it makes me
more efficient.

55
00:04:32,936 --> 00:04:37,232
{\an8}A regular reminder of the passage of time
seems to have a subtle but definite...

56
00:04:37,357 --> 00:04:40,068
{\an8}- I'll turn the volume down.
- Thank you.

57
00:04:42,654 --> 00:04:45,491
{\an8}It's not me.
They're testing the fire-alarm system.

58
00:04:45,616 --> 00:04:47,326
They'll be working on it
for the next couple days.

59
00:04:47,451 --> 00:04:48,702
Great!

60
00:04:53,373 --> 00:04:56,335
{\an8}We got an I.D. on the guy
who was killed by the bomb.

61
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:59,546
David Ronaldo, 47 years old,
from Hillsboro.

62
00:04:59,671 --> 00:05:01,131
His family there's
been notified.

63
00:05:01,256 --> 00:05:03,509
Does it confirm Jane's idea
that he was the original target?

64
00:05:03,634 --> 00:05:05,427
Turns out he's connected
to an old murder.

65
00:05:06,595 --> 00:05:10,599
{\an8}14 years ago, a woman was killed
by a guy named Luis Cruz.

66
00:05:10,724 --> 00:05:12,893
He busted into her house
and shot her.

67
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:15,103
He's been on death row
for the past 11 years.

68
00:05:15,229 --> 00:05:16,563
{\an8}How's Ronaldo connected?

69
00:05:16,688 --> 00:05:19,316
{\an8}For some reason, Ronaldo thought
Cruz was innocent.

70
00:05:19,441 --> 00:05:20,901
He's been trying
to get him exonerated.

71
00:05:21,026 --> 00:05:23,028
- How does that get you killed?
- You got me.

72
00:05:23,153 --> 00:05:26,073
{\an8}Abbott's gone down to the west Livingston
death row unit to talk to Cruz

73
00:05:26,198 --> 00:05:28,116
see if he can shed some light
on the situation.

74
00:05:28,242 --> 00:05:29,576
He left already?
What's the hurry?

75
00:05:29,701 --> 00:05:32,329
Cruz is sentenced to be executed
in three days.

76
00:05:40,128 --> 00:05:41,547
{\an8}No, not David.

77
00:05:43,882 --> 00:05:45,676
I'm afraid so, Mr. Cruz.

78
00:05:48,804 --> 00:05:52,558
{\an8}- It ain't right. There's no sense to it.
- How did you know Mr. Ronaldo?

79
00:05:52,683 --> 00:05:56,103
{\an8}We, uh...
We grew up together.

80
00:05:58,564 --> 00:06:01,650
{\an8}His folks were my... my foster parents
From the time I was 8.

81
00:06:01,775 --> 00:06:04,945
{\an8}David and me,
we were brothers, tight.

82
00:06:07,614 --> 00:06:10,576
{\an8}I should've been more like him,
like David.

83
00:06:11,118 --> 00:06:14,329
{\an8}He was a good student,
good son, good brother.

84
00:06:15,747 --> 00:06:18,125
{\an8}But I was...
I was a mess.

85
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:21,211
{\an8}First narcotics arrest
was at the age of 16, right?

86
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:24,673
David would bail me out
help me get into a program or whatever.

87
00:06:24,798 --> 00:06:28,927
{\an8}Then I'd screw things up
and back I'd go.

88
00:06:29,052 --> 00:06:31,680
Recently, he was trying to get
your conviction overturned.

89
00:06:31,805 --> 00:06:34,558
{\an8}He'd been working with my...
with my lawyer for years

90
00:06:35,183 --> 00:06:38,395
{\an8}spending his own money,
tracking down any...

91
00:06:39,521 --> 00:06:41,690
{\an8}Any crazy thing
that might help an appeal.

92
00:06:42,441 --> 00:06:45,485
Mr. Cruz, have you heard
from him recently?

93
00:06:45,903 --> 00:06:47,863
{\an8}I got a message
a few days ago

94
00:06:48,655 --> 00:06:50,490
saying that he... he might have
some good news

95
00:06:50,616 --> 00:06:52,868
{\an8}but he didn't want to say
until he was sure.

96
00:06:54,286 --> 00:06:57,080
- And he never mentioned what it was?
- No.

97
00:06:57,205 --> 00:06:59,416
You have any idea of problems
he may have had?

98
00:06:59,625 --> 00:07:02,294
{\an8}Girlfriend problems,
money issues?

99
00:07:02,419 --> 00:07:03,503
Never mentioned any.

100
00:07:04,630 --> 00:07:05,881
I appreciate your time.

101
00:07:13,555 --> 00:07:16,600
{\an8}Agent, I didn't kill that woman.

102
00:07:19,353 --> 00:07:21,104
{\an8}I don't expect you
to do anything.

103
00:07:22,814 --> 00:07:24,691
I just need somebody
to believe me.

104
00:07:27,694 --> 00:07:29,821
All the things
I've done in my life

105
00:07:30,322 --> 00:07:31,823
I never killed anybody.

106
00:07:33,909 --> 00:07:35,535
I'd appreciate it
if you'd just...

107
00:07:36,203 --> 00:07:37,663
Just try to believe that.

108
00:07:43,835 --> 00:07:44,878
Thanks.

109
00:07:55,722 --> 00:07:57,057
{\an8}What have we got, kids?

110
00:07:57,557 --> 00:08:00,394
{\an8}Found an atm with a camera
across the street from the explosion.

111
00:08:00,519 --> 00:08:04,898
There's no video but it automatically
takes a still picture every second, so...

112
00:08:05,565 --> 00:08:08,276
that's about a minute and a half
before it happened.

113
00:08:08,527 --> 00:08:11,738
Then about 20 seconds later,
our bomber appears.

114
00:08:11,863 --> 00:08:13,949
Techs confirmed that's the briefcase
the bomb was in.

115
00:08:14,074 --> 00:08:15,742
- Can you enhance the image?
- I already did

116
00:08:15,867 --> 00:08:18,078
but the disguise makes him
impossible to identify.

117
00:08:18,203 --> 00:08:20,789
- Facial recognition can't do anything.
- That's quite the rug.

118
00:08:21,039 --> 00:08:24,710
Then the mom and baby appear...

119
00:08:26,086 --> 00:08:28,463
the bus pulls in,
bus pulls out

120
00:08:28,588 --> 00:08:29,923
and...

121
00:08:31,091 --> 00:08:33,260
- Boom!
- Whoa, whoa.

122
00:08:33,677 --> 00:08:36,680
Okay, can you back it up a ways?
Before the bus pulls in?

123
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:41,768
- The briefcases... they're identical.
- Can you enlarge that?

124
00:08:43,562 --> 00:08:45,522
See, I don't think
that's a coincidence.

125
00:08:46,898 --> 00:08:49,026
Okay, can... can you pull up an image

126
00:08:49,484 --> 00:08:51,987
before the explosion
but after the bus pulled in?

127
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:55,282
There's only one briefcase now

128
00:08:55,532 --> 00:08:57,159
so the bomber
must have taken Ronaldo's.

129
00:08:57,284 --> 00:08:59,244
Leaving Ronaldo
with the bomb at his feet.

130
00:08:59,870 --> 00:09:02,039
So, the bomber
not only wanted Ronaldo dead

131
00:09:02,164 --> 00:09:04,708
- he wanted what was in that briefcase.
- Exactly.

132
00:09:06,293 --> 00:09:09,254
Look at that woman.
Can... can you back her up a bit?

133
00:09:10,380 --> 00:09:11,423
You see?

134
00:09:11,548 --> 00:09:14,468
She's there on purpose to distract Ronaldo
from the briefcase.

135
00:09:14,593 --> 00:09:16,720
You find that woman,
you will find your bomber.

136
00:09:19,181 --> 00:09:20,849
Try facial recognition.

137
00:09:37,741 --> 00:09:39,785
Gina Petrocelli, open up!

138
00:09:40,702 --> 00:09:42,996
- Ms. Petrocelli!
- "Yes?"

139
00:09:43,246 --> 00:09:45,707
- FBI. Open the door.
- "Okay."

140
00:09:45,832 --> 00:09:47,584
"I just got out of the shower.
I need a minute."

141
00:09:47,834 --> 00:09:49,419
You have 30 seconds.

142
00:09:49,544 --> 00:09:53,840
No problem. Just need to dry off.
What's this about, anyway?

143
00:09:53,965 --> 00:09:56,301
20 seconds and we're coming in,
Ms. Petrocelli.

144
00:09:56,426 --> 00:09:58,970
Okay, just...
Don't shoot or anything.

145
00:09:59,679 --> 00:10:01,223
Just need to put
some pants on.

146
00:10:01,348 --> 00:10:03,350
Those pants look all right to me, Gina.

147
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:10,816
- This your baby?
- Yeah.

148
00:10:12,025 --> 00:10:13,276
Cute.

149
00:10:15,195 --> 00:10:17,531
Gina, you've had run-ins
with the law before...

150
00:10:17,656 --> 00:10:18,990
Arrests for fraud.

151
00:10:19,741 --> 00:10:22,285
I think you know
this is far worse than that.

152
00:10:22,410 --> 00:10:25,247
I didn't know anything
about the bomb. I swear.

153
00:10:25,705 --> 00:10:27,541
No idea that was gonna happen.

154
00:10:28,917 --> 00:10:30,210
You know who this is?

155
00:10:30,919 --> 00:10:32,462
Yes. Absolutely.

156
00:10:32,712 --> 00:10:35,423
His name is Peter Koch.
He's a private investigator.

157
00:10:35,549 --> 00:10:38,135
- I help him out sometimes.
- I'll have Wylie run him.

158
00:10:38,677 --> 00:10:40,137
What do you mean
"help him out"?

159
00:10:40,262 --> 00:10:43,098
When there's something
he needs to get... Creatively.

160
00:10:43,223 --> 00:10:45,308
- You mean illegally.
- Whatever.

161
00:10:46,184 --> 00:10:48,603
Pete throws me some money.
I do what he asks me to do.

162
00:10:48,728 --> 00:10:50,230
Why did he kill
David Ronaldo?

163
00:10:50,355 --> 00:10:52,023
I don't know anything
about that.

164
00:10:52,232 --> 00:10:55,068
All Pete told me is he needed
help snatching a briefcase.

165
00:10:55,193 --> 00:10:57,904
I distract the Mark.
He makes the Grab. Easy.

166
00:10:58,071 --> 00:10:59,531
Then I'm walking away,
and... boom...

167
00:10:59,656 --> 00:11:02,242
I'm face down on the sidewalk,
I can't hear

168
00:11:02,367 --> 00:11:03,910
people around me
are bleeding.

169
00:11:04,828 --> 00:11:06,746
You think I would get into that
on purpose?

170
00:11:07,455 --> 00:11:09,291
He didn't tell you
anything about it?

171
00:11:10,542 --> 00:11:14,629
No. Just, uh...
That it was a big job.

172
00:11:15,672 --> 00:11:17,215
He was gonna make
a lot of money.

173
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:19,885
New development.
Pete Koch is dead.

174
00:11:23,305 --> 00:11:26,516
Local P.D. found Koch's body
in an alley in east Austin

175
00:11:26,641 --> 00:11:29,436
about four hours ago...
gunshot to the head.

176
00:11:29,644 --> 00:11:32,856
His watch and wallet were missing
so they assumed it was a robbery gone bad.

177
00:11:33,273 --> 00:11:34,608
Any signs of the briefcase?

178
00:11:34,733 --> 00:11:37,527
None. And we had his apartment searched.
It wasn't there, either.

179
00:11:38,028 --> 00:11:40,947
I'd say that rules out robbery.
What about Gina Petrocelli?

180
00:11:41,573 --> 00:11:44,826
This one, I think she's telling the truth.
She didn't know about the bomb

181
00:11:44,993 --> 00:11:47,245
and she doesn't know
who hired Koch to get the briefcase.

182
00:11:47,370 --> 00:11:51,416
Okay. Charge Petrocelli with conspiracy
and whatever else you can think of.

183
00:11:51,541 --> 00:11:53,627
- Anything else?
- No, that's it.

184
00:11:57,214 --> 00:11:58,340
Jane.

185
00:12:03,345 --> 00:12:04,554
Do you know what this is?

186
00:12:04,679 --> 00:12:07,766
I'm betting it's a transcript
from Luis Cruz's trial for murder.

187
00:12:08,183 --> 00:12:10,268
- Good read?
- It's a little dry.

188
00:12:10,977 --> 00:12:12,354
Cruz was a drug addict.

189
00:12:12,979 --> 00:12:15,649
The night of the murder
he said that he was alone, using.

190
00:12:16,399 --> 00:12:17,692
No one to confirm it.

191
00:12:17,984 --> 00:12:21,238
The D.A. had circumstantial evidence
and a piece of Luis' clothing

192
00:12:21,363 --> 00:12:24,532
with the dead woman's blood on it.
The blood clinched it.

193
00:12:24,658 --> 00:12:26,910
Yes, well, blood's very easy to plant.

194
00:12:27,035 --> 00:12:29,537
- Childishly easy, really.
- Yes, it is.

195
00:12:29,788 --> 00:12:31,539
You don't think Cruz did it? Why?

196
00:12:31,665 --> 00:12:34,834
Well, it's just a feeling
that I can't shake.

197
00:12:35,585 --> 00:12:36,878
What do you think?

198
00:12:37,003 --> 00:12:40,131
Well, David Ronaldo was looking
for evidence to clear Cruz.

199
00:12:40,257 --> 00:12:43,051
He was killed and had
something stolen from him.

200
00:12:43,176 --> 00:12:46,179
That pretty strongly suggests
that he was onto something.

201
00:12:47,013 --> 00:12:48,515
Which means that,
in the next few days

202
00:12:48,640 --> 00:12:51,768
the state of Texas is going
to execute an innocent man.

203
00:12:52,185 --> 00:12:54,020
And once he's dead

204
00:12:54,145 --> 00:12:56,481
there's very little incentive
to reopen the case...

205
00:12:57,232 --> 00:12:58,817
Which the real killer knows.

206
00:13:00,652 --> 00:13:01,695
So...

207
00:13:02,988 --> 00:13:04,948
What are we gonna do
about all this?

208
00:13:05,073 --> 00:13:07,409
Oh, well, I think I can come up
with something.

209
00:13:12,412 --> 00:13:18,209
14 years ago, Luis Cruz was accused
of murdering this woman... Sarah Feinberg.

210
00:13:18,334 --> 00:13:21,629
She was shot to death in her home
apparently by an intruder.

211
00:13:21,754 --> 00:13:25,550
Her body was found
by her husband, Edward Feinberg.

212
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:29,137
Edward was a psychiatrist
at the university teaching hospital.

213
00:13:29,721 --> 00:13:32,890
He found his wife's body
when he arrived home later that night.

214
00:13:33,016 --> 00:13:34,934
Anything connecting Cruz
to the Feinbergs?

215
00:13:35,059 --> 00:13:37,812
Yes.
Edward Feinberg knew Cruz

216
00:13:37,937 --> 00:13:41,190
from a halfway-house drug-treatment
program that Feinberg ran.

217
00:13:41,316 --> 00:13:43,318
Cruz and Feinberg
had an argument

218
00:13:43,985 --> 00:13:47,613
because Cruz showed up to a therapy
session high three days before the murder.

219
00:13:47,739 --> 00:13:50,825
And the D.A. claimed that that made
Cruz kill Sarah Feinberg?

220
00:13:50,992 --> 00:13:53,453
- That's thin.
- What put Cruz at the murder scene?

221
00:13:53,578 --> 00:13:56,497
They found a sweatshirt of Cruz's
snagged on a bush outside of the house

222
00:13:56,622 --> 00:13:58,291
with Sarah Feinberg's blood
on it.

223
00:13:58,416 --> 00:14:00,251
There was also gunpowder
on one sleeve.

224
00:14:00,376 --> 00:14:02,086
- What about the gun?
- They didn't find it.

225
00:14:02,211 --> 00:14:03,713
"It's 10:00."

226
00:14:04,672 --> 00:14:07,258
- I'll turn it off.
- No worries. It's good to know.

227
00:14:07,383 --> 00:14:08,468
10:00.

228
00:14:08,593 --> 00:14:11,220
We believe that David Ronaldo
was murdered

229
00:14:11,346 --> 00:14:14,223
because he had evidence
that would clear Luis Cruz.

230
00:14:15,016 --> 00:14:16,893
We need to find out
what that was.

231
00:14:17,018 --> 00:14:21,397
And because they're going
to execute Cruz in about... 50 hours

232
00:14:22,565 --> 00:14:25,818
- we should hurry up.
- Boss, is there any evidence

233
00:14:25,943 --> 00:14:28,613
that the cops or the prosecutors
made a mistake?

234
00:14:28,738 --> 00:14:31,741
- Not that I can point to.
- And this case was fully appealed, right?

235
00:14:32,075 --> 00:14:35,244
- Yes.
- Then can I ask why we're taking this on?

236
00:14:35,745 --> 00:14:37,288
I don't want to sound cold

237
00:14:37,413 --> 00:14:39,832
but it seems to me that the system did
what it was supposed to do.

238
00:14:39,957 --> 00:14:43,044
No, it didn't.
Because Luis Cruz is innocent.

239
00:14:43,586 --> 00:14:44,879
You're absolutely certain
of that?

240
00:14:45,004 --> 00:14:46,297
Well, I'm not absolutely certain
of anything.

241
00:14:46,422 --> 00:14:49,092
But I'm confident enough to know
that I'd like to try and help.

242
00:14:49,550 --> 00:14:51,386
- That's good enough for me.
- Me too.

243
00:14:53,096 --> 00:14:55,640
Okay.
Let's do it.

244
00:14:55,765 --> 00:14:57,767
Fischer, Cho, work Ronaldo.

245
00:14:57,892 --> 00:15:00,061
Go to his apartment,
see if you can find anything.

246
00:15:00,186 --> 00:15:02,605
I'll call the governor's office
and let him know what we're up to.

247
00:15:02,855 --> 00:15:05,817
Jane, Lisbon, talk to the Feinbergs.
Wylie...

248
00:15:09,529 --> 00:15:12,698
just keep letting us know
what time it is.

249
00:15:15,993 --> 00:15:18,079
{\an8}Really? Well, we're just...
just getting here now.

250
00:15:20,039 --> 00:15:23,793
I can't do anything tonight.
This case could go all hours.

251
00:15:25,795 --> 00:15:28,798
Thursday's great.
Okay. Me too.

252
00:15:29,215 --> 00:15:30,466
Bye, Marcus.

253
00:15:34,804 --> 00:15:35,847
What?

254
00:15:36,222 --> 00:15:37,682
Nothing. Nothing.

255
00:15:38,724 --> 00:15:40,518
- Mr. Feinberg?
- Yes.

256
00:15:41,227 --> 00:15:42,645
You're from the FBI?

257
00:15:42,770 --> 00:15:44,939
Yes, I'm agent Lisbon.
This is Patrick Jane.

258
00:15:46,149 --> 00:15:47,358
Come in.

259
00:15:58,202 --> 00:16:00,663
- This is my wife, Mae.
- Nice to meet you.

260
00:16:00,788 --> 00:16:02,331
Sit, please, sit.

261
00:16:06,836 --> 00:16:08,004
Now...

262
00:16:08,504 --> 00:16:12,300
What exactly are you here for? You said
on the phone it had to do with Luis Cruz.

263
00:16:12,467 --> 00:16:16,053
Yes. His friend David Ronaldo was killed.
Have you ever heard of him?

264
00:16:16,179 --> 00:16:17,847
- I don't think so.
- No.

265
00:16:17,972 --> 00:16:20,933
And what does his death
have to do with us?

266
00:16:21,058 --> 00:16:22,518
It's complicated.

267
00:16:25,938 --> 00:16:28,441
Well, that's...
That's a little evasive, now, isn't it?

268
00:16:28,774 --> 00:16:32,403
This is an ongoing investigation.
I'm not allowed to say more.

269
00:16:32,904 --> 00:16:35,740
This is the room where
your wife was killed, right?

270
00:16:36,240 --> 00:16:37,325
Yes.

271
00:16:37,450 --> 00:16:40,411
- You have an office here, too.
- Yes, I see patients here.

272
00:16:40,536 --> 00:16:41,996
That must be kind of weird.

273
00:16:42,121 --> 00:16:43,789
You like to provoke,
now, don't you?

274
00:16:44,540 --> 00:16:46,000
Classic defense mechanism.

275
00:16:47,251 --> 00:16:53,090
And, yes, it was, as you say, "weird"
for some time.

276
00:16:53,216 --> 00:16:56,802
But I wasn't going to let Cruz
chase me out of my own home.

277
00:16:56,928 --> 00:16:59,263
Well, I'm not judging, honestly.
I'm just curious.

278
00:16:59,388 --> 00:17:01,974
And I must say, I love what
you've done with the remodeling.

279
00:17:02,099 --> 00:17:04,310
- Oh, we did it together.
- I detected your touch.

280
00:17:04,435 --> 00:17:06,020
How long have you two
been married?

281
00:17:07,438 --> 00:17:09,440
Oh, almost 11 years.

282
00:17:10,024 --> 00:17:12,443
And did you know each other
at the time of the murder?

283
00:17:12,735 --> 00:17:15,279
No. I was a new
graduate student then.

284
00:17:15,404 --> 00:17:18,950
Edward was far too important
to notice me.

285
00:17:19,992 --> 00:17:25,456
I went through a period of deep grieving
for several years after Sarah was killed.

286
00:17:25,873 --> 00:17:27,542
But I pulled myself out of it

287
00:17:27,667 --> 00:17:31,712
and we were fortunate enough
to, uh... find each other.

288
00:17:31,837 --> 00:17:33,673
So, it was fate, huh?
Wonderful.

289
00:17:33,798 --> 00:17:36,092
A couple more questions
before we go.

290
00:17:36,342 --> 00:17:37,802
Have you ever considered suicide?

291
00:17:39,011 --> 00:17:41,138
- No.
- And you, Mae...

292
00:17:41,264 --> 00:17:43,766
Did your father go to your
high-school graduation ceremony?

293
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:45,893
No.

294
00:17:46,018 --> 00:17:47,395
What does that have to do
with anything?

295
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:49,564
You'd be surprised.
Thank you for your time.

296
00:17:49,689 --> 00:17:52,817
Before we go, do you think I could
just quickly use your bathroom?

297
00:17:53,276 --> 00:17:54,402
Yeah, of course.

298
00:17:54,527 --> 00:17:57,363
- It's through the kitchen, on your right.
- Thank you.

299
00:18:38,362 --> 00:18:40,114
One last question.
Just curious...

300
00:18:40,239 --> 00:18:43,117
How long before the first Mrs. Feinberg
died did you start your affair?

301
00:18:44,994 --> 00:18:48,164
Fascinating method you have
if it is a method.

302
00:18:48,789 --> 00:18:52,001
Mae and I did not have an affair
before my wife died.

303
00:18:52,126 --> 00:18:56,964
Really? You're so careful to mention
your "deep grieving period," even now.

304
00:18:57,089 --> 00:19:00,176
And, you know, this is not really the house
of someone that's sentimental...

305
00:19:00,301 --> 00:19:01,385
quite the opposite.

306
00:19:02,303 --> 00:19:03,929
You're very observant, Mr. Jane.

307
00:19:05,014 --> 00:19:07,808
And perhaps you're right.
I do protest too much.

308
00:19:08,309 --> 00:19:11,354
But I have grown weary and defensive
on the subject

309
00:19:11,479 --> 00:19:13,939
- as I'm sure you might understand.
- I do, I do.

310
00:19:14,065 --> 00:19:16,651
I understand that Luis Cruz
didn't kill your wife. You did.

311
00:19:17,151 --> 00:19:18,486
And we're gonna prove it.

312
00:19:19,362 --> 00:19:21,656
Unless you want to confess now
and save us all some time.

313
00:19:22,073 --> 00:19:24,867
Now, I assumed that this was where
this was all building toward.

314
00:19:25,076 --> 00:19:28,204
You're not the first to think it.
I was her husband, after all.

315
00:19:28,704 --> 00:19:31,415
- But it's not true.
- Leave my husband alone.

316
00:19:32,792 --> 00:19:34,210
I'd like you both
to leave now.

317
00:19:34,335 --> 00:19:36,337
- Now.
- Yes. Of course, ma'am. Yes. Jane?

318
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:48,724
Ronaldo did not have much of a life
outside the Cruz case.

319
00:19:49,183 --> 00:19:50,309
Guess not.

320
00:19:51,185 --> 00:19:52,687
- This is weird.
- What?

321
00:19:53,187 --> 00:19:55,439
Everything taped to the walls
is old except for this map.

322
00:19:55,564 --> 00:19:58,067
Looks brand-new.
What's it doing here?

323
00:19:58,693 --> 00:19:59,860
Got me.

324
00:20:00,903 --> 00:20:02,655
Let's make sure
we take it back with us.

325
00:20:05,282 --> 00:20:07,702
Hey, did Lisbon date
on the job at CBI?

326
00:20:07,827 --> 00:20:09,286
Don't know.
Never asked.

327
00:20:09,620 --> 00:20:12,081
Surprising to see her and Pike
hook up, huh?

328
00:20:12,998 --> 00:20:14,458
Wouldn't have put
those two together.

329
00:20:16,961 --> 00:20:18,421
You ever wonder
what Jane thinks about it?

330
00:20:18,546 --> 00:20:20,506
- Never.
- Really? Why not?

331
00:20:20,631 --> 00:20:22,091
My brain would explode.

332
00:20:33,644 --> 00:20:34,854
Hey, Cho.

333
00:20:35,563 --> 00:20:37,773
Does Ronaldo seem like the type
who'd go diving?

334
00:20:39,525 --> 00:20:42,069
Ronaldo started taking diving
lessons about a month ago.

335
00:20:42,194 --> 00:20:45,239
Diving lessons? Why?
Was he planning on going to the ocean?

336
00:20:45,614 --> 00:20:47,992
- Not that we could tell.
- Well, isn't it obvious?

337
00:20:49,076 --> 00:20:50,453
He was looking for the gun.

338
00:20:50,578 --> 00:20:51,912
- He was?
- Sure.

339
00:20:52,037 --> 00:20:54,498
Well, the police still haven't found the gun
that killed Sarah Feinberg.

340
00:20:54,623 --> 00:20:58,586
This map shows all the bodies of water
within a 50-mile radius of the crime scene.

341
00:20:58,753 --> 00:21:03,048
Ronaldo planned on diving them all
one by one in the hopes of finding it.

342
00:21:03,174 --> 00:21:05,384
And why would he think the gun
was thrown into a body of water?

343
00:21:05,885 --> 00:21:07,636
I don't know, but he did.

344
00:21:07,762 --> 00:21:10,681
And that is why the killer
wanted his briefcase...

345
00:21:10,806 --> 00:21:12,933
To see how far Ronaldo
had gotten with his plan.

346
00:21:13,058 --> 00:21:14,935
That's a pretty desperate move
on Ronaldo's part.

347
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:17,855
I think "desperate" more or less
describes the whole situation.

348
00:21:17,980 --> 00:21:19,940
Well, we got 20 hours.
What are we supposed to do?

349
00:21:20,065 --> 00:21:21,358
We're right where
we want to be.

350
00:21:21,650 --> 00:21:23,194
I'm gonna need some help
from Wylie

351
00:21:23,319 --> 00:21:26,655
and we need to set up a security station
downstairs in the lobby.

352
00:21:26,781 --> 00:21:28,073
- Why?
- Well, we are the FBI.

353
00:21:28,199 --> 00:21:29,533
Shouldn't we be secure?

354
00:21:29,658 --> 00:21:32,995
If we arrest Feinberg,
he has to stay here, right?

355
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:34,663
He can't just up and leave.

356
00:21:34,955 --> 00:21:37,917
- Just overnight, until his arraignment.
- Overnight is perfect.

357
00:21:39,543 --> 00:21:40,836
What are we arresting him for?

358
00:21:51,013 --> 00:21:52,348
- Yes?
- Edward Feinberg?

359
00:21:52,473 --> 00:21:55,142
- Yes.
- Special agent Cho, FBI.

360
00:21:55,434 --> 00:21:56,602
You're under arrest, sir.

361
00:21:57,436 --> 00:21:58,938
- For what?
- Prescription drug fraud.

362
00:21:59,063 --> 00:22:01,398
We believe you've been selling pads
like this one to drug users.

363
00:22:01,524 --> 00:22:03,609
- Where did you get that?
- Turn around, sir.

364
00:22:04,902 --> 00:22:10,199
- Edward? What's going on?
- I don't know. Call Rich Summers.

365
00:22:10,324 --> 00:22:12,576
- Have him meet me at the FBI office.
- I'll follow you.

366
00:22:22,628 --> 00:22:24,755
Let the games begin.

367
00:22:32,177 --> 00:22:34,054
Empty your pockets.
Take off your watch, please.

368
00:22:35,180 --> 00:22:37,057
- When will I get these back?
- Tomorrow.

369
00:22:37,182 --> 00:22:38,559
When you leave
for your arraignment.

370
00:22:38,684 --> 00:22:39,852
Your purse and cellphone.

371
00:22:39,977 --> 00:22:41,687
I'm not under arrest.
Why should I give you my purse?

372
00:22:41,812 --> 00:22:43,856
- FBI policy.
- Well, that's crazy.

373
00:22:44,147 --> 00:22:45,190
You're free to leave at any time.

374
00:22:45,315 --> 00:22:47,276
But if you want to stay,
your purse and cellphone, please.

375
00:22:47,651 --> 00:22:50,195
You should go.
The attorney's on his way.

376
00:22:50,320 --> 00:22:52,155
No. Of course not.
I'm staying with you.

377
00:22:52,698 --> 00:22:54,032
Step through, please.

378
00:23:04,209 --> 00:23:05,502
Wait here, please.

379
00:23:18,140 --> 00:23:20,893
- "It's 5:00."
- Excuse me?

380
00:23:21,018 --> 00:23:23,437
Sorry. It's just...
Never mind.

381
00:23:23,562 --> 00:23:24,730
You might want to cover
your ears.

382
00:23:24,855 --> 00:23:26,189
Why would
I want to do that?

383
00:23:26,899 --> 00:23:28,650
They're testing
the fire alarm!

384
00:23:28,775 --> 00:23:31,111
It goes off twice a day,
at 9:00 and 5:00!

385
00:23:32,738 --> 00:23:34,781
It's getting pretty annoying,
actually.

386
00:23:35,032 --> 00:23:38,118
Agent Collins, show Mr. Feinberg
into interrogation.

387
00:23:38,243 --> 00:23:39,620
Someone will be in shortly.

388
00:23:43,040 --> 00:23:45,208
- Can't I go with him?
- No, I'm sorry.

389
00:23:45,334 --> 00:23:47,669
You'll have to stay in the waiting room.
Come with me.

390
00:24:01,183 --> 00:24:02,935
You should be comfortable
enough in here.

391
00:24:03,060 --> 00:24:04,645
Bathrooms are around the corner.

392
00:24:05,145 --> 00:24:07,522
I have to stay in here?
For how long?

393
00:24:08,440 --> 00:24:12,110
I can show you to your own
interrogation room If you'd prefer.

394
00:24:14,071 --> 00:24:15,530
No. That's fine.

395
00:24:34,841 --> 00:24:35,968
Hello.

396
00:24:37,552 --> 00:24:40,889
- What time is it?
- 8:17.

397
00:24:41,014 --> 00:24:44,393
- Sorry to keep you waiting so long.
- Nonsense.

398
00:24:44,518 --> 00:24:48,021
You kept me waiting on purpose
like you trumped up this drug charge.

399
00:24:48,230 --> 00:24:50,899
Last, desperate attempt
to prove your pet theory.

400
00:24:52,359 --> 00:24:54,569
You know, it must be very difficult
for people around you

401
00:24:54,695 --> 00:24:56,571
the fact you can
never admit to being wrong.

402
00:24:57,614 --> 00:24:58,657
Okay.

403
00:24:59,157 --> 00:25:00,867
Well, let's cut to the chase,
shall we?

404
00:25:00,993 --> 00:25:05,163
In 14 hours, Luis Cruz is gonna be
executed for a crime that you committed.

405
00:25:07,165 --> 00:25:09,167
I know you think
you're doing the right thing.

406
00:25:09,292 --> 00:25:11,586
I mean, it's always the husband, right?

407
00:25:12,295 --> 00:25:14,715
- But in this case, it's not.
- So you've said.

408
00:25:14,840 --> 00:25:16,425
Fine. Believe what you want.

409
00:25:17,300 --> 00:25:19,302
- I didn't murder my wife.
- Yes, you did.

410
00:25:19,428 --> 00:25:21,263
Then you got in your car and
you drove back to your office.

411
00:25:21,388 --> 00:25:23,390
You stopped on the way
and you dumped the gun.

412
00:25:23,515 --> 00:25:24,891
Now, you could have
dumped it anywhere

413
00:25:25,017 --> 00:25:27,811
but you chose to dump it in a lake,
which is a good idea.

414
00:25:27,936 --> 00:25:29,062
Great hiding spot.

415
00:25:29,563 --> 00:25:32,315
Of course, until David Ronaldo
figured it out.

416
00:25:32,441 --> 00:25:34,151
Ronaldo got your goat,
didn't he?

417
00:25:34,276 --> 00:25:35,736
I don't even know
what you mean.

418
00:25:35,861 --> 00:25:38,447
Well, I mean that he wasn't nearly
as clever as you

419
00:25:38,572 --> 00:25:40,574
but he was determined.

420
00:25:41,283 --> 00:25:44,286
His persistence really paid off
for him, didn't it?

421
00:25:44,536 --> 00:25:46,621
- Well, you tell me.
- I will.

422
00:25:47,039 --> 00:25:50,375
That's why you hired the private
detective Koch to keep an eye on him.

423
00:25:50,792 --> 00:25:54,087
And you had him killed once
he started taking diving lessons.

424
00:25:54,504 --> 00:25:56,631
Then you had Koch
go through his briefcase

425
00:25:56,757 --> 00:26:00,927
to see what evidence he had
and then you killed Koch.

426
00:26:01,470 --> 00:26:03,221
This is a very interesting theory.

427
00:26:04,097 --> 00:26:07,434
But even if you find the gun
it's not gonna prove that I'm guilty.

428
00:26:07,559 --> 00:26:09,686
Well, it's not gonna have
any fingerprints on it, of course

429
00:26:09,811 --> 00:26:12,898
but it may still have
serial numbers on it

430
00:26:13,023 --> 00:26:16,234
and you would be surprised
what the FBI can do with serial numbers.

431
00:26:16,359 --> 00:26:20,113
And, of course, it does prove
Luis Cruz's innocence

432
00:26:20,739 --> 00:26:23,492
because the timeline suggests
that there was no way

433
00:26:23,617 --> 00:26:25,619
that he could dump
the gun anywhere

434
00:26:25,744 --> 00:26:28,747
between your house and your office
before he was arrested.

435
00:26:29,331 --> 00:26:32,125
Well, it looks like you've got
your work cut out for you now, doesn't it?

436
00:26:37,255 --> 00:26:40,092
My name is Richard Summers.
I'm Mr. Feinberg's attorney.

437
00:26:40,217 --> 00:26:43,053
I'll be sitting in for the rest
of the interrogation.

438
00:26:43,178 --> 00:26:46,139
Well, I hope for your sake, Richard
that you're charging him by the hour

439
00:26:46,264 --> 00:26:49,309
- 'cause it's gonna be a long night.
- Here's how this is gonna work.

440
00:26:50,018 --> 00:26:53,730
You can keep my client here overnight
but he's not gonna speak to you.

441
00:26:53,897 --> 00:26:54,940
- About anything.
- Richard...

442
00:26:55,065 --> 00:26:59,861
Not about the prescription-drug matter
not about the murder case. Nothing.

443
00:27:00,612 --> 00:27:02,447
- Are we clear?
- Crystal.

444
00:27:02,823 --> 00:27:05,283
Now I'd like a moment alone
with my client.

445
00:27:18,880 --> 00:27:20,715
Cho, you're up.

446
00:27:31,017 --> 00:27:32,644
I'm gonna ask you
some questions.

447
00:27:33,770 --> 00:27:36,439
You can ask anything you want.
He's not gonna answer you.

448
00:27:36,565 --> 00:27:37,983
I want to hear it from him.

449
00:27:38,650 --> 00:27:41,111
"Where were you on the night
of February 11, 2000?"

450
00:27:46,408 --> 00:27:50,537
"I know this is hard, but I need to talk to
you about the night your wife died."

451
00:27:50,662 --> 00:27:52,581
"Any little detail could help."

452
00:27:53,206 --> 00:27:55,125
"But first, let's get you
something to drink"

453
00:27:57,711 --> 00:27:58,920
"Let's just chat."

454
00:27:59,880 --> 00:28:01,506
"How do you like U.T. next year?"

455
00:28:01,631 --> 00:28:04,176
"I mean I don't know
about their running game."

456
00:28:17,772 --> 00:28:20,692
Well, I hear
the cat's got your tongue.

457
00:28:21,234 --> 00:28:22,736
You think you'll let something slip?

458
00:28:24,487 --> 00:28:25,655
Okay.

459
00:28:27,073 --> 00:28:28,575
I'm gonna ask you again.

460
00:28:29,326 --> 00:28:32,454
Where were you on the night
of February 11, 2000

461
00:28:32,579 --> 00:28:35,373
in between the hours
of 6:00 and 9:00 p.m.?

462
00:28:35,498 --> 00:28:37,876
- Richard?
- Is this necessary?

463
00:28:38,376 --> 00:28:41,922
- You know he's not gonna discuss it.
- Yes, it's necessary

464
00:28:42,047 --> 00:28:44,299
because in seven hours,
a man is gonna die.

465
00:28:44,424 --> 00:28:46,468
- You know what? I resent...
- Ed! Don't.

466
00:28:50,388 --> 00:28:52,182
Kim, what are you doing?

467
00:28:52,307 --> 00:28:53,850
You're not going to get anything
out of him tonight.

468
00:28:53,975 --> 00:28:55,977
- You might as well let him rest.
- No.

469
00:28:56,895 --> 00:29:01,233
- Dennis. You need to rest.
- Fine.

470
00:29:02,859 --> 00:29:04,861
Don't get comfortable.
I'll be back.

471
00:29:07,656 --> 00:29:09,199
I'll be turning out the lights,
Mr. Summers

472
00:29:09,324 --> 00:29:12,911
but you are welcome to stay.
Only one pillow, though.

473
00:29:13,036 --> 00:29:15,538
And hopefully the floor
is okay with you.

474
00:29:15,664 --> 00:29:17,874
I'm sure it's been cleaned sometime
in the past year.

475
00:29:18,583 --> 00:29:20,543
- Let's hope.
- Richard, you should go.

476
00:29:20,752 --> 00:29:22,254
I want you rested
for my arraignment.

477
00:29:22,379 --> 00:29:24,339
- Are you sure?
- I will not say another word

478
00:29:24,464 --> 00:29:28,343
- until you get back. I promise.
- Good. Get some rest.

479
00:29:43,191 --> 00:29:45,151
Hi, there.
I brought you this.

480
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:49,322
Is this from the FBI handbook?
Some good cop/bad cop thing?

481
00:29:50,907 --> 00:29:53,159
No, it's just a pillow
and a blanket.

482
00:29:53,285 --> 00:29:55,328
Do you have any idea what
you're doing to my husband?

483
00:29:55,453 --> 00:29:59,457
You're asking him to relive
a very painful chapter in his life.

484
00:29:59,582 --> 00:30:01,334
I think it was more painful for Sarah.

485
00:30:02,752 --> 00:30:06,339
Anyway, it'll all be over soon enough.
6 1/2 hours, to be exact.

486
00:30:06,464 --> 00:30:08,508
Yeah, then we'll finally have closure,
no thanks to you.

487
00:30:09,009 --> 00:30:12,637
Now, if you'll excuse me,
I would like to get some rest.

488
00:30:15,307 --> 00:30:17,726
You needn't be a martyr.
Take the pillow.

489
00:30:54,888 --> 00:30:58,224
- Good morning, sleepyhead.
- What time is it?

490
00:30:59,934 --> 00:31:01,436
It's a little after 9:00.

491
00:31:01,936 --> 00:31:03,563
Your murderous ass
got to take a nap

492
00:31:03,688 --> 00:31:05,815
while all the good guys
had to stay up all night.

493
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:10,695
- I take it you didn't find the gun.
- No.

494
00:31:11,112 --> 00:31:13,782
So that gives me 40 minutes
to get you to talk.

495
00:31:14,699 --> 00:31:16,409
- I want my attorney.
- We'll call him.

496
00:31:16,534 --> 00:31:20,080
- But first, the truth.
- I have nothing to say.

497
00:31:24,542 --> 00:31:25,627
We'll see.

498
00:31:29,881 --> 00:31:31,508
"It's 9:30."

499
00:31:32,592 --> 00:31:34,886
I'd like to know how much longer
you plan on keeping us here.

500
00:31:35,011 --> 00:31:36,679
You're free to go at any time.

501
00:31:37,097 --> 00:31:40,266
But your husband will remain in custody
until his arraignment.

502
00:31:41,518 --> 00:31:43,395
Look, I know this is hard on you.

503
00:31:44,187 --> 00:31:47,524
- You want a cup of coffee?
- Yes, thank you.

504
00:31:48,441 --> 00:31:50,235
I'm checking in
with the dive unit.

505
00:31:51,111 --> 00:31:53,405
Damn it. Tell them
they've got 30 minutes.

506
00:31:54,697 --> 00:31:55,824
Nothing yet.

507
00:31:55,949 --> 00:31:57,325
You're gonna talk to me!

508
00:31:58,368 --> 00:32:00,703
Please. I-I want my attorney.

509
00:32:00,829 --> 00:32:03,832
In the next few minutes
Luis Cruz is going to die for your crime.

510
00:32:04,416 --> 00:32:07,544
Does that mean anything to you?
A man's death?

511
00:32:07,836 --> 00:32:11,047
Is that all you've got?
Appeals to my sympathy?

512
00:32:11,172 --> 00:32:12,674
Answer the question!

513
00:32:13,425 --> 00:32:16,177
If he were innocent,
it would be a tragedy. But he's not.

514
00:32:16,386 --> 00:32:19,305
He killed my wife,
my beautiful, sweet Sarah.

515
00:32:19,431 --> 00:32:22,308
He deserves to die.
I look forward to it.

516
00:32:23,184 --> 00:32:24,602
There you go.

517
00:32:31,151 --> 00:32:32,444
Fischer.

518
00:32:35,989 --> 00:32:37,866
He's not talking.
It's all over.

519
00:32:37,991 --> 00:32:39,325
You called
the governor's office?

520
00:32:39,451 --> 00:32:40,910
I spoke to an aide.
No go.

521
00:32:41,035 --> 00:32:42,954
Without a weapon,
they're not gonna postpone.

522
00:32:43,079 --> 00:32:44,122
Crap.

523
00:32:44,581 --> 00:32:46,749
And he made it
abundantly clear

524
00:32:46,875 --> 00:32:49,627
that once Cruz is executed,
the case is dead.

525
00:32:50,211 --> 00:32:52,130
They're not gonna want to hear
one more word about it.

526
00:32:53,465 --> 00:32:54,507
So, what now?

527
00:32:54,632 --> 00:32:57,010
Come on.
Everyone's watching it on tv.

528
00:33:17,489 --> 00:33:19,991
I'm sorry, people.
I wish this happened a different way.

529
00:33:22,410 --> 00:33:24,037
Boss. Look.

530
00:33:39,511 --> 00:33:40,845
We did what we could.

531
00:33:56,234 --> 00:33:57,485
Congratulations.

532
00:33:58,861 --> 00:34:00,112
For what?

533
00:34:00,780 --> 00:34:02,573
Luis Cruz is dead.

534
00:34:17,588 --> 00:34:19,048
Well, thank you for telling me.

535
00:34:20,299 --> 00:34:22,176
Doesn't feel the way
you thought it would, does it?

536
00:34:22,301 --> 00:34:23,469
No.

537
00:34:24,637 --> 00:34:27,682
No, I thought I'd feel avenged,
or at least relieved.

538
00:34:29,517 --> 00:34:31,185
Instead it's just sad.

539
00:34:32,937 --> 00:34:35,064
And it doesn't bring
my wife back.

540
00:34:36,732 --> 00:34:38,109
No. It doesn't.

541
00:34:40,236 --> 00:34:41,862
I'm not supposed to be
talking to you.

542
00:34:42,154 --> 00:34:44,073
And I don't even know
why I should want to.

543
00:34:44,198 --> 00:34:46,033
- You think I'm a murderer.
- Oh, I don't know.

544
00:34:46,158 --> 00:34:48,202
I know you didn't kill your wife.
I knew all along.

545
00:34:49,245 --> 00:34:53,165
- What?
- Well, I strongly suspected you didn't.

546
00:34:53,332 --> 00:34:55,918
And your reaction
to Cruz's death proves it.

547
00:34:56,043 --> 00:34:58,087
Not the reaction of a guilty man.

548
00:35:00,339 --> 00:35:03,509
- Your wife, Mae, however...
- What about Mae?

549
00:35:03,634 --> 00:35:06,220
- Oh, come on.
- I don't know what you're talking about.

550
00:35:06,345 --> 00:35:07,972
I'll let you see for yourself.

551
00:35:15,938 --> 00:35:18,733
This is her learning of the execution.

552
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:30,703
Well, that doesn't mean anything.

553
00:35:30,828 --> 00:35:33,080
- Come on, you can see it. It's obvious.
- No, it's not obvious.

554
00:35:33,205 --> 00:35:35,833
Well, if you didn't do it and Luis Cruz
didn't do it, who did it?

555
00:35:35,958 --> 00:35:38,836
- Who wanted to kill your wife?
- I-I don't... I don't know.

556
00:35:38,961 --> 00:35:40,713
Mae worked at the clinic, right?

557
00:35:40,838 --> 00:35:43,090
Very easy for her to get a piece
of his clothing.

558
00:35:43,215 --> 00:35:45,217
"She drove to your house,
shot your wife"

559
00:35:45,343 --> 00:35:47,470
"and left his sweatshirt
at the scene."

560
00:35:47,595 --> 00:35:50,097
"Then she dumped the gun
in the lake on the way home."

561
00:35:50,389 --> 00:35:51,766
No, no, no, no,
no, no, no, no.

562
00:35:51,891 --> 00:35:53,559
She knew you were never gonna
leave your wife

563
00:35:53,684 --> 00:35:55,686
so she took care of it for you.

564
00:35:57,772 --> 00:35:59,732
She was your student, your lover.

565
00:36:00,274 --> 00:36:02,860
She idolized you. She was
obsessed, and you knew that.

566
00:36:03,402 --> 00:36:05,279
But you enjoyed
the devotion.

567
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:07,323
It fed your ego

568
00:36:07,448 --> 00:36:10,242
so you didn't allow yourself
to look too closely.

569
00:36:10,368 --> 00:36:13,329
- And now an innocent man is dead.
- I don't believe any of this.

570
00:36:13,454 --> 00:36:14,538
And she was the one
who convinced you

571
00:36:14,664 --> 00:36:17,375
to keep the affair a secret
after your wife died, right?

572
00:36:21,003 --> 00:36:22,129
Yes.

573
00:36:22,254 --> 00:36:24,799
Why do you think she stayed here
all night last night?

574
00:36:25,675 --> 00:36:27,301
To support you? No.

575
00:36:28,678 --> 00:36:30,388
Because she's scared.

576
00:36:30,513 --> 00:36:32,723
She wants to know
what we have.

577
00:36:33,265 --> 00:36:37,144
Mr. Feinberg, we're dropping the charges
against you. You're free to go.

578
00:36:37,812 --> 00:36:40,189
There's no point now.
We can't make a case.

579
00:36:40,314 --> 00:36:41,857
Edward, thank God.

580
00:36:54,829 --> 00:36:56,205
Edward, wait!

581
00:36:57,206 --> 00:36:59,750
- Edward, what's wrong? Why are you ignor...
- Let me have the keys.

582
00:37:00,793 --> 00:37:02,294
Well, what did they say?

583
00:37:03,462 --> 00:37:05,423
- Edward, speak to me.
- Give me the keys!

584
00:37:14,974 --> 00:37:17,810
Mae, I have a question for you
and you need to give me an honest answer.

585
00:37:18,018 --> 00:37:19,603
You can ask me anything.

586
00:37:20,855 --> 00:37:26,527
- Did you kill Sarah?
- What? Who told you that?

587
00:37:26,652 --> 00:37:29,613
- I need to know. Was it you?
- Of course not.

588
00:37:30,156 --> 00:37:32,158
I would die rather than hurt you.

589
00:37:34,201 --> 00:37:35,494
I don't believe you.

590
00:37:36,078 --> 00:37:37,580
- Don't say that.
- It was you.

591
00:37:37,705 --> 00:37:40,374
- Edward, stop! It's not true!
- You knew I'd never leave her.

592
00:37:40,499 --> 00:37:43,753
W... I lo... I love you. That doesn't mean
that I would kill the woman...

593
00:37:43,878 --> 00:37:46,422
- Don't lie to me!
- Please. You're scaring me.

594
00:37:46,547 --> 00:37:48,007
This is insane.
You are insane.

595
00:37:48,132 --> 00:37:50,259
- You don't understand!
- Then explain it to me!

596
00:37:50,384 --> 00:37:51,969
I did it for us!

597
00:37:56,766 --> 00:37:58,851
- So we could be together.
- Oh, my God.

598
00:37:59,977 --> 00:38:01,437
Oh, my God.
How could you have done this?

599
00:38:01,562 --> 00:38:03,230
You have to believe me.
It was the only way.

600
00:38:03,355 --> 00:38:06,984
And it worked. We're together.
Now that he's dead, we are safe.

601
00:38:07,151 --> 00:38:09,153
There is no one looking
for the killer.

602
00:38:09,487 --> 00:38:11,697
- We have weathered the...
- Don't touch me.

603
00:38:14,700 --> 00:38:17,703
We have weathered the storm.
We should be celebrating.

604
00:38:26,712 --> 00:38:28,798
Get out of the car!
Now!

605
00:38:33,803 --> 00:38:35,721
I don't understand.
What's going on?

606
00:38:35,846 --> 00:38:39,099
You're under arrest for the murder
of David Ronaldo, Sarah Feinberg

607
00:38:39,225 --> 00:38:41,769
- And Peter Koch.
- But Luis Cruz killed Sarah.

608
00:38:41,894 --> 00:38:45,147
- I mean, he was just executed for it.
- Well, actually, he wasn't.

609
00:38:45,272 --> 00:38:47,691
But they said on the news...
it happened at 10:00.

610
00:38:49,527 --> 00:38:51,695
8:30. I don't understand.

611
00:38:54,615 --> 00:38:56,158
- You tricked me?
- Bingo.

612
00:38:56,492 --> 00:38:58,494
You took our phones, his watch.

613
00:38:58,619 --> 00:39:02,540
"We changed all the clocks
including your car and cellphones"

614
00:39:02,790 --> 00:39:04,333
Actually, you woke up at 7:00.

615
00:39:05,251 --> 00:39:07,378
The TV... they did that, too?

616
00:39:07,503 --> 00:39:10,506
Oh, no. That was agent Wylie.
I'm not much with computers.

617
00:39:10,631 --> 00:39:13,300
He took some footage from earlier
in the day and put a little banner on it.

618
00:39:13,425 --> 00:39:15,594
I did, however, plant the bug
in your husband's pocket

619
00:39:15,719 --> 00:39:18,305
that recorded everything
that you said in the car.

620
00:39:21,642 --> 00:39:25,479
This is FBI agent Dennis Abbott.
I need to speak to the governor.

621
00:39:25,688 --> 00:39:27,106
Feeling a little sleepy?

622
00:39:29,567 --> 00:39:31,944
Mae Feinberg gave up
the location of the gun.

623
00:39:34,029 --> 00:39:36,615
It was in a pond off of I-35.

624
00:39:38,367 --> 00:39:39,952
So that's what David
was looking for?

625
00:39:40,619 --> 00:39:41,662
Yes.

626
00:39:42,663 --> 00:39:44,582
We wouldn't have been able
to find it without him.

627
00:39:49,837 --> 00:39:51,463
He died trying to get me
out of here.

628
00:39:51,755 --> 00:39:52,923
He did.

629
00:39:55,676 --> 00:39:58,178
Lawyers are doing their thing.
It'll take a little while.

630
00:39:58,304 --> 00:40:00,139
But this proves
you're innocent, Luis.

631
00:40:01,599 --> 00:40:03,225
You'll be walking
out of here soon.

632
00:40:21,869 --> 00:40:22,912
How'd it go?

633
00:40:23,579 --> 00:40:25,080
Oh, well...

634
00:40:26,957 --> 00:40:28,375
It's not every day
you get to tell a man

635
00:40:28,500 --> 00:40:30,669
that he's getting off death row
and going home.

636
00:40:31,587 --> 00:40:34,131
- It was emotional.
- I can imagine.

637
00:40:34,715 --> 00:40:36,550
Oh, you're gonna like this.

638
00:40:36,675 --> 00:40:39,637
We found out how Ronaldo knew
to look for the gun in a pond.

639
00:40:40,095 --> 00:40:41,805
He went to a psychic.

640
00:40:41,931 --> 00:40:44,308
She said she saw it
near a body of water.

641
00:40:44,433 --> 00:40:46,894
Please. Come on.
How many times do I have to say it?

642
00:40:47,019 --> 00:40:49,813
There's no such thing
as psychics.

643
00:40:49,939 --> 00:40:51,357
And it's the oldest one
in the book.

644
00:40:51,482 --> 00:40:56,195
I mean, if you think about it
most things are close to a body of water.

645
00:40:56,320 --> 00:40:58,030
You're just mad
because she was right.

646
00:40:58,155 --> 00:41:01,951
Even a broken clock
gets the chance to be right twice a day.

647
00:41:10,459 --> 00:41:11,669
Horse pucky.

648
00:41:19,051 --> 00:41:20,970
- Look at you.
- Don't start.

649
00:41:21,095 --> 00:41:22,763
I'm not starting.
You look beautiful.

650
00:41:24,181 --> 00:41:26,058
I hope he's taking you
someplace nice.

651
00:41:26,558 --> 00:41:28,310
Cloth napkins
and everything.

652
00:41:28,769 --> 00:41:29,853
Fancy.

653
00:41:30,771 --> 00:41:32,231
Well, you have a great time.

654
00:41:34,108 --> 00:41:35,401
Good night, Jane.

655
00:41:41,365 --> 00:41:42,533
Night, Teresa.

